#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46af019b-5e0e-54fe-a3a4-d3a5f79204d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440035} {:answer/id #uuid "36089e95-fc88-5a76-b2c4-311f215a41a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440035} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7bba9ed-822d-53ee-89c9-116fde41ee52" :answer-field/key "$k=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440375} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "242f0892-e1e1-5e47-ac02-f10209cc7691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440037} {:answer/id #uuid "5261eeed-b902-50cd-be8c-e339e0cd7603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440038} :answer-field/id #uuid "3baa83a4-cb6e-5db3-8f0f-7d1c5b2abfae" :answer-field/key "$k=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440376} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8201e6-2d8f-5889-8d5a-7f26e45965c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440039} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa3b90b-7a80-5418-b7a0-778bc241d10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440040} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab487ccf-fee0-51d3-aff0-dfee470b09fd" :answer-field/key "$k=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11af43ea-89bf-58ea-b458-4433226aa764" :question/math-academy-id "q-9913" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix  $\\begin{bmatrix}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & k\\end{vmatrix} & =k\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & k\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =k(3k-6)+0+0 \\\\  & =k(3k-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0.$ So,  we have:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(3k-6) & =0 \\\\ 3k(k-2) & =0 \\\\ k & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1875e854-1900-58b4-bea8-ec0e5d6629b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-9t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398230} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca1dcb5-771f-59eb-81e9-1e595c34e462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398231} {:answer/id #uuid "676387be-6610-5f16-9796-2babd5ce0f03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-5t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398232} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbb623b-497e-5583-a206-85cecb99534f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398233} {:answer/id #uuid "a43b3ff9-d167-5f2a-a49d-342a3fc83921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398234}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398230} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d025741-a121-5bf5-a673-350aefe4b1cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "757cf839-4729-500e-9a5f-1ad15eb1165b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122527" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-4,2).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=1-\\frac{3y}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2-3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2-3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2-3t,\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2x-3y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2-3t)-3t \\\\  & =4-9t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x-3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-9t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed1a1ab0-6d18-5f30-b80b-be9f46dd80b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398363} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cbc8d4-c2ee-5478-860e-c731a5274aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398364} {:answer/id #uuid "f499f957-93d0-5ca6-8e65-57c931e33b8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398365} {:answer/id #uuid "d7aa6514-a3ad-578c-8808-8284836b7b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398366} {:answer/id #uuid "431e58b2-3a4b-54f5-a2fc-05d378bf57ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398365} :answer-field/id #uuid "530f90fa-b927-569c-b4dd-79831f2442af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "da3be12b-b270-5258-9b1c-409842422d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-131116" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d89472a-c8a6-58ee-ad06-702bb85242bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{u}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398509} {:answer/id #uuid "6ceed292-fdb3-5c81-a5ef-e21d4577bafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398510} {:answer/id #uuid "2e773ddf-4b3c-5bc1-b538-c1702b36529f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398511} {:answer/id #uuid "06d6268a-1fe9-5430-934f-527eb0153af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5{u}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398512} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b5545b-f437-5265-821f-6ea217aea603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{u}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398512} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48bed04-e645-581d-90b2-d43b8caed324" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50f21d4c-115f-5f6b-a6fe-50fa77f7169e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161251" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨3x,\\,3y,\\,z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{u}^{2}⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨3x,\\,3y,\\,z⟩{|}_{x=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,z={u}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\\,u⟩ \\\\  & =3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)+3u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\cdot (-2{u}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v)+{u}^{2}\\cdot u \\\\  & =-6{u}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡v+{sin}^{2}⁡v)+{u}^{3} \\\\  & =-5{u}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,-5{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f13cf51-1f17-54c3-b3d0-3a4ed9f6cc76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398536} {:answer/id #uuid "0823e1c6-a66c-59c3-bb60-2b11710017b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u{v}^{2}-{u}^{2}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398537} {:answer/id #uuid "7a803047-69b8-53fa-8d05-7893832957a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}{v}^{2}-2uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398538} {:answer/id #uuid "3330207b-8637-52ec-b488-bd1a488b22dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u{v}^{2}+uv+2{u}^{2}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398539} {:answer/id #uuid "915a87fb-c1cd-571b-be39-7c31f1761641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398540} :answer-field/id #uuid "af2f16f5-115b-5596-b71d-8a4dab14117a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd17085b-48fe-5be2-bc57-b62ce4e45b8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161383" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨xz,\\,y,\\,xz⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,uv,\\,v⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨v,\\,-1,\\,u⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨v,\\,-1,\\,u⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨xz,\\,y,\\,xz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y=uv\\,z=v} \\\\  & =⟨uv,\\,uv,\\,uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨uv,\\,uv,\\,uv⟩\\cdot ⟨v,\\,-1,\\,u⟩ \\\\  & =uv\\cdot v+uv\\cdot (-1)+uv\\cdot u \\\\  & =u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,u{v}^{2}-uv+{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc0eea45-e863-58d6-862a-727382209bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398949} {:answer/id #uuid "f143a8ac-50ee-51ef-b268-61d02a927238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398950} {:answer/id #uuid "997b1e10-3a89-5f14-bd4d-db6ba1a97e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398951} {:answer/id #uuid "da9bac7a-0578-5611-970c-aaa5ef3c56ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398952} {:answer/id #uuid "b37aff02-8b26-5092-a1c6-e4bfb88a16b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398953} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b666e0-c6c9-5c9c-b88e-9912d89e0283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b0863b6f-83a0-5fff-bd6b-d5b930ec7673" :question/math-academy-id "q-71396" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4643060a-8d1d-5789-a362-102d70382db9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} {:answer/id #uuid "8070bb66-d16c-505c-85ee-2a417bc81d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398957} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdcaf70-4358-5973-a594-026557c83288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398958} {:answer/id #uuid "61e68fe8-0332-53d0-b214-d38a39b52899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398959} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c9c882-083f-5978-8947-be71bba2f10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398956} :answer-field/id #uuid "91095769-6695-5735-bdf7-d8d4bfdca38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58a67c4a-aa8b-5b64-8268-56928e2716de" :question/math-academy-id "q-71399" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{x+y+z}+x+y+z)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }j+({e}^{x+y+z}+1)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,0) & =({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,i+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,j+({e}^{0+0+0}+1)\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399510 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d35582-3a44-5c08-8455-d2f9e84525d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399512} {:answer/id #uuid "aba5b46d-91ae-5e56-a685-c83b09b8b31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399513} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d5554c-9aa3-5296-9ebc-642d7ab8668d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399514} {:answer/id #uuid "21ab22a6-9eb5-5460-a37a-51dabf9c5da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=36\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399515} {:answer/id #uuid "a8369c3d-7d61-5b31-87ce-5b1b018ff986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399514} :answer-field/id #uuid "36c47bf3-1ccf-54fd-84c2-b2131105d199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "23823644-896c-5b5c-b67a-e94d42442783" :question/math-academy-id "q-106391" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b8b0f54b4bc1e9c7362bd0b22f3dbc2550594855a1225350d366b9a86b0e3f0.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399531 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b497d42e-bb82-5ad8-b534-e2c147e1ff42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399533} {:answer/id #uuid "0713f8eb-12ff-5fe2-9526-65877bb581ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399534} {:answer/id #uuid "3969d63a-a2eb-506f-beb7-6cde82a0d05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399535} {:answer/id #uuid "9a825ff3-cad9-54b1-8ffe-6c0d2f47c301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399536} {:answer/id #uuid "da3c9fdb-6c6a-55f0-8a89-dc38f3583e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399537} :answer-field/id #uuid "031319c8-f560-5ad6-9024-95c6acad5a42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "df313a83-5c0b-5be0-b850-d3d38af97ca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-106706" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b77fa9e3a398cf9f6cc3a3ea163b83cb75f1e5c9ea98aa1ae34730616c47977c.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ffa0ff-df0b-5bc5-b896-3f6772a35019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$28$ and $24$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399852} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1177f-3e11-593f-ad9c-dda3299fba50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$24.5$ and $18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399853} {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} {:answer/id #uuid "ee829b75-97f1-545e-8cf4-3704340f4ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$49$ and $36$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399855} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e390e1-9f85-5321-a954-3f147b97e82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$7$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399856}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} :answer-field/id #uuid "44102d96-20a4-51fc-add2-7a7a026df6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3113828e-6ca6-530b-804d-2cdc91aade73" :question/math-academy-id "q-115134" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "869e00c6-a4ba-5e49-adbb-6c7b2dfda6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437800} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3eef7f-38ce-53c9-b7d7-5ad7331d9db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437800} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee1ffe9f-6a37-5717-a79c-006e1e279e9f" :answer-field/key "$det(F)=-7$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73f233cd-a07f-56cc-b019-5bf1a281c9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437802} {:answer/id #uuid "0370b8e5-fbed-53fa-bba4-73a7be7e3de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437803} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e6ecc2-f970-55e9-9644-03d1a8d33b21" :answer-field/key "$F$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecabd635-8703-591e-8d3a-ae23bbdf9467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437804} {:answer/id #uuid "a6071a93-94cd-5aa5-98a0-dee30b1fa3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437804} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b2f581-bf3c-5b63-a3e5-a1336a490a06" :answer-field/key "$det(F)=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438931} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09770f28-1851-55a6-907e-48f97d04ec38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669019} {:answer/id #uuid "01aca0ce-8957-55c8-8d7a-37e49a101f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186669019} :answer-field/id #uuid "a948a82a-6898-532e-87b0-ae1a9d0da2ea" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "af5c321a-ef93-52c0-aab1-818de4211e7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-128478" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $F$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nF=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-8\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(2-3)-8(-1-5)+3(-3-10) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-8\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot (-13) \\\\  & =-2+48-39 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(F)=7$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$F$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a30e54a5-5549-54be-a77d-0271a747f483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437806} {:answer/id #uuid "1678c617-1408-5d9c-b2b6-f33cb2bcbc97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437807} :answer-field/id #uuid "8934c094-53e8-5252-811d-16f83b72799e" :answer-field/key "$D$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdeb7450-ec19-5575-89aa-66f56497533b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437808} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4c6b03-6a3d-5e71-8ee1-bd76a8e47d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437808} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7edd53d-df96-5e60-b073-b95bd4d92abf" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438933} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0668d434-29bc-5c22-ae50-ede25ab3fb55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437810} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d7c2a7-df8c-5627-999f-6d43c26ca389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437811} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7f7e987-6e7f-5d92-a6ad-a4d71c321aa2" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04472571-0a00-5fac-9502-9b8f8afd8c8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128480" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(4-15)+3(-2+9)+1(-5+6) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-11)+3\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-22+21+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is a singular matrix” and $det(D)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400359 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5b8b793-b0e3-5e04-8a37-a42568d9a5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437818} {:answer/id #uuid "20bd5256-a3ef-5a48-b762-66cfb4a56832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437818} :answer-field/id #uuid "f79065b5-83e0-5c45-91ca-02a92fe83586" :answer-field/key "$S$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438938} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e1d911-6963-56d2-a197-b7ef0fd454a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437820} {:answer/id #uuid "cee5556a-e5f1-5649-8414-f96934145291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437821} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d2d1bc7-850d-5cc3-a700-a5297c1412e2" :answer-field/key "$S$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c31c4506-e579-5c13-bf9e-df8c0b4217ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437822} {:answer/id #uuid "f7553a7c-6618-5039-9f2e-70225d0f69b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437822} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd92868f-3a2b-5764-95b0-634de408c963" :answer-field/key "$det(S)=5+2\\sqrt{7}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5bcc7af8-2cd5-53ed-a962-12d9bd009077" :question/math-academy-id "q-128491" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $S$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{7} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\sqrt{7} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{7} & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{7}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & 0 \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{7}(2\\sqrt{7}-0)-0+\\frac{1}{2}(0-10) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{7}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{7}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-10) \\\\  & =28-5 \\\\  & =23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=23\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not**  singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$S$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3601e09a-9335-5956-9835-0fbca8ffb502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439483} {:answer/id #uuid "3db08d97-0845-5f7c-ab43-56777bea1d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439483} :answer-field/id #uuid "36f65856-78a1-51a9-a1da-71cda1e674f1" :answer-field/key "$m=-6$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440099} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dec92b27-277d-5920-9c63-50c355350d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439485} {:answer/id #uuid "d7efe730-22a8-5253-b96e-23123e4a98e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439485} :answer-field/id #uuid "21524ea9-5072-5e34-9765-c48f94050d16" :answer-field/key "$m=-5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440100} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a17a4ff-d40c-5630-a3e9-10db02ad93e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439487} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf556fa-4d79-5443-8bfa-93c96686c0b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439488}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439487} :answer-field/id #uuid "1274cfce-7367-5750-bf06-dd8f1d1cfeec" :answer-field/key "$m=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440101} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6367235-10eb-5b6e-8e95-119de9f56fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439489} {:answer/id #uuid "b943d1ca-7cab-5262-81fa-8f183158d7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439489} :answer-field/id #uuid "53e76782-4383-593d-b1d2-b6c475ceb4a9" :answer-field/key "$m=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440102} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c370c082-b2ae-5038-9133-5e429cbafa66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439491} {:answer/id #uuid "e077bebf-4ed8-550b-a674-b8c67d696044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439492} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d73edd5-cd07-5400-bca8-9dc482987051" :answer-field/key "$m=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440103} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48620b4b-6598-5913-ad54-44caf8e83b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439493} {:answer/id #uuid "2e884f18-53fd-5041-94e9-ff9a8b0abf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439494} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b49d521-d2dd-5bd3-a65b-f63b17f6768a" :answer-field/key "$m=8$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30a80f14-b653-5ec9-a12d-20d43bb40f91" :question/math-academy-id "q-128506" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & m \\\\ m & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis $-29$, find all the possible values of $m.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 6 & m \\\\ m & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-6\\begin{vmatrix}m & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+m\\begin{vmatrix}m & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3-0)-6(m-0)+m(m-6) \\\\  & =3-6m+{m}^{2}-6m \\\\  & ={m}^{2}-12m+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-29,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-12m+3 & =-29 \\\\ {m}^{2}-12m+32 & =0 \\\\ m & =\\frac{-(-12)\\pm \\sqrt{{12}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 32}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm 4}{2} \\\\  & =6\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=4$ and $m=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5c6186e-dc96-51d6-82cf-5c90683fa84d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439503} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd21dd5-0725-5fc9-a948-255a7375e687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439504}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439504} :answer-field/id #uuid "aae52bae-eb88-5ee0-a6c0-ddf96c9c5413" :answer-field/key "$(-3,-3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f138de-76d3-51c6-b70f-37f4ae000dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439505} {:answer/id #uuid "abd8a918-fae9-5155-b1e4-9387ec218bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439505} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea60b4f-c7d5-5ba9-b54b-a47a64f292ec" :answer-field/key "$(-3,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "707f2204-105d-5f98-a7c5-02aaedc7a057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439507} {:answer/id #uuid "d8586660-fd7e-5eda-9284-01ae5a7a40b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439507} :answer-field/id #uuid "192ed7e0-867a-5042-9244-595a5145fa06" :answer-field/key "$(3,-3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440111} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7553f1-861f-51c0-8547-71189b4ae228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439509} {:answer/id #uuid "3f812865-94f7-5778-aa60-73b89c449256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439509} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd2c2d4-80d6-5681-8a2f-d724cd733e7a" :answer-field/key "$(3,3)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a633d445-62c8-5864-b7bf-d694cd1cd56c" :question/math-academy-id "q-128519" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x,$ find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇g(x,y)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3f9a2e3-bee8-5b1b-b1c5-43ca27a9ee8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439511} {:answer/id #uuid "f628d092-8784-5d3d-b54f-458bd5beb768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439512}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439511} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87136b4-f54f-5a06-86b5-cf63e992b1d1" :answer-field/key "$(-2,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12fafdc0-e4b2-52e6-ae9c-99cc3b27a59b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439513} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b5298a-472b-5545-8b69-e594bf8766ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439514} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d688553-f546-5705-b1b5-e8fa688d5ccc" :answer-field/key "$(-2,2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440114} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d89573c1-b7fc-57e0-b937-a941c611e3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439515} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2526f8-dbfa-54f5-953e-68b316490ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439516} :answer-field/id #uuid "0da4e8cd-a37a-575d-b740-45d4e50ce9a5" :answer-field/key "$(0,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440115} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcee5b74-6097-5de5-a2f9-1ef377001ba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439517} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9ac649-f4ee-5a5e-aa1e-4df3984df146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439517} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9513575-9539-55dd-8564-2815f12cfbf6" :answer-field/key "$(0,2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6851bbc7-80d6-5dc9-8a00-f559624fa12f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130217" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2y-2x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $-i+j?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (-i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f14d31-90ea-5326-97dc-45022d0835ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u+v-2w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401277} {:answer/id #uuid "b740b568-485c-5311-a423-33ea2f3eeffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the elliptic cylinder ${u}^{2}+2{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401278} {:answer/id #uuid "a6795027-5482-5bff-8214-2dd2cc097fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401279} {:answer/id #uuid "5adbc1f1-362c-537c-bb50-2cdecc09b123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$ plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401280} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401281} :answer-field/id #uuid "65676948-b2fa-585d-8619-ca89f50c6f34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d8752c18-2285-5bd2-a3b2-ba4d0420bd04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161814" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401284} {:answer/id #uuid "10a3627e-14c0-553d-8005-b8e65337d0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=-2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401285} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffd8d2-f852-57f7-9970-8454309f0281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $2u+v+w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401286} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fe9b5d-4fbe-56d7-9627-c5c1df64a715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401287} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cbb05e-806b-56aa-8136-04b088a6dc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401284} :answer-field/id #uuid "175d9200-8100-5e54-b984-b7451df1967c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fc380b3f-d92e-5c45-8651-fac240787b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161816" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e70215a-59ba-5fc4-8869-08fd079debf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401304} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6baa46-2362-5360-8c5f-7031d62e1411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401305} {:answer/id #uuid "7adad8b8-d3ef-52ef-b487-e4018ac1507b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401306} {:answer/id #uuid "06bed278-63d1-5662-b5c3-13bc65e1ffc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401307} {:answer/id #uuid "03678aa6-e1ae-5bec-8d2a-faf8e1de8ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(uv)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401304} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b627816-1293-53a9-a8e8-93bf2fb0fcef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4004275e-59e4-54d2-bbaa-29203ac29308" :question/math-academy-id "q-161879" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z-3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69eaa3fe-4c03-532c-9d1a-cd84b8703ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{4}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401338} {:answer/id #uuid "460112aa-e36b-52a6-a02b-783fee9d05b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{xy}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401339} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fbcee-821a-5cd9-8a6e-f9412bd4ab5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401340} {:answer/id #uuid "e12cffd5-0b22-5b0b-a3e3-7270fff61900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} {:answer/id #uuid "1540b931-3add-5e5c-99b4-77aa9c86e5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401341} :answer-field/id #uuid "17de6bba-7478-572d-9fe3-ada91a6e9edc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a28026ac-9427-5db7-b40f-31513274adc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162365" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x=\\pm 1,$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the surface $z=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our surface $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\right)=\\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=1/({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{x}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-x\\cdot \\frac{-2x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-y\\cdot \\frac{-2y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/b2/b215ec1e3330f96e7e606e085721b2dd1f50d113f0151e36ec98e6d0a6f54e73.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,-\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc634ad5-e010-5c6c-accf-cdd1bfc87d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401359} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee290fe-8425-5228-94c2-57bd0a2da175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=(xy)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401360} {:answer/id #uuid "726bbe70-cba3-547c-bf90-2310b15873c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401361} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3e8097-9217-5fb9-b7d3-fa8eb7bed46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401362} {:answer/id #uuid "1354e106-6bc4-5c0a-9398-0cd9067aa706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401360} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c61b134-7c57-530f-8b97-97a808179f81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d5fedbb-f6c9-5abe-b595-3219e3d242a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162517" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y\\,i-2x{y}^{2}\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y\\,i-2x{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=-2x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4xy+(-4xy)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}=(xy)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755a881c-6b14-5e96-bd68-6af58d5ef813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401539} {:answer/id #uuid "5965e390-56a0-55ef-a7dc-eff34c09cc53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401540} {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1fc5c2-bdfa-52e9-a59e-c6227d171448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401542} {:answer/id #uuid "47a16fa9-9d49-55d0-a143-c2b6cca5c4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41dd28d-0ca0-5222-9cfe-700ddaa37f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e791f5b6-5e18-534a-99e5-45cc16c1809f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16455" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=1$ and ${b}^{2}=8.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=1,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=8,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=1.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402609 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f93a90-917d-59e1-ad77-0b9aad541d3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438266} {:answer/id #uuid "12af740c-a997-5387-8362-5e2cc16732ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438266} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b6fcdaf-3dd3-5706-a56b-12ba3e57c541" :answer-field/key "$N$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439162} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80660670-28fa-562c-9cdd-803d3f3b6e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438268} {:answer/id #uuid "5834e804-b776-587a-9eff-2c1a6107cc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438269} :answer-field/id #uuid "676ac767-8dfd-50fa-9ecf-b51ab8fab0b5" :answer-field/key "$N$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439163} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d97f56-3c64-5231-b1a7-ace7dd7012d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438270} {:answer/id #uuid "1c459c6d-1c91-560e-9b29-0f2524d0be20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438270} :answer-field/id #uuid "091267d0-c528-519a-8b1c-763f1c12a047" :answer-field/key "$det(N)=-1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a5eb8a6b-37c6-54e6-b340-18101dbf3f8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38187" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & 3 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ 5 & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & -\\frac{1}{7}\\end{vmatrix}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} & 4 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7} & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(-\\frac{4}{7}+\\frac{5}{7})-3(-\\frac{1}{49}+\\frac{1}{49})-\\frac{1}{7}(\\frac{5}{7}-\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}-3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{49}-\\frac{1}{49} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$N$ is a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e017db58-a75d-5917-9421-bf22d82c2b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403316} {:answer/id #uuid "b5cf3476-c2f7-54d9-a5ee-5be3cfa06980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403317} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9f49c8-1095-5a46-8358-a01d4a4ae113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403318} {:answer/id #uuid "345e3ffa-dba9-5031-b6d1-880d3b6973b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403319} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9dd63b-07cb-5b7e-a9ff-84a037aad39b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403320} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c978983-f26a-5475-8ce3-d86bb0940128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403315}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "99d45658-64c1-527b-ba91-3ee0b91560a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-6880" :question/problem "![](images/86/86dcfa13b7863c08b348855e776a6df709ecac499af02c2479cfdf484eeaf328.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=4$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8bfe8db-1989-5e4c-9ad9-c6db83ad7bb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438484} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7cbeaf-dee8-5b96-8308-94729708799c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438484} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec23c8b-291b-5610-9226-611cabeda584" :answer-field/key "$T$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439271} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f42e4a93-c186-58e5-951e-917759693098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438486} {:answer/id #uuid "f52e2b00-e5d8-52b7-83e8-eba250bef166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438486} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb6943e-42d8-5697-b023-3106b7e5611c" :answer-field/key "$det(T)=-3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439272} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74d5746f-c69f-5b13-8c52-ab7a0cb272bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438488} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a8ba1d-5bcb-54c6-a41a-1a451759a5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438489} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6d01c9-2762-59a1-a7a5-cb52ffab3689" :answer-field/key "$T$ is **not** a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0582f249-3bf5-5079-8fe1-8ed6df521a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-69835" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(2+3)-2(1+6)-1(-1+4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5-2\\cdot 7-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =20-14-3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(T)=3\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$T$ is **not** a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74e84be6-fb21-5888-bde0-2b1fc8861800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨-3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403470} {:answer/id #uuid "125b86ba-8a17-5cc7-bb9b-688c88040c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨3,-4,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403471} {:answer/id #uuid "a563a551-487d-52b0-b121-65d93d53f35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403472} {:answer/id #uuid "74e61dbd-b0b0-51fb-aee1-a797c08c8fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403473} {:answer/id #uuid "65d0bd41-c0f7-5813-a9d5-53b92a3b0d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403474} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a86f3ea-971f-5c0c-ab0d-7a86359ca0e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca7dad4d-8007-5c21-9019-38d66b5366c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71407" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b0c95e4-c6e9-54b6-a15d-ea7087127b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439909} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e5d812-4735-5fc8-b852-eeaeeaafc9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439910}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439909} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d2c8b7-ca4d-57e5-9519-e48520596596" :answer-field/key "$(-1,-1,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440312} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8aaf15a-211e-5baf-b019-f56f2c0415e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439911} {:answer/id #uuid "5d99844b-afc9-55be-ac3f-aa4cbca61e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439912} :answer-field/id #uuid "11d89f4b-da53-5a2f-a038-31630954b391" :answer-field/key "$(-1,-1,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45c4460b-1ba4-50d7-8521-b02dc00d9262" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439913} {:answer/id #uuid "a6df45b3-5daa-5782-88d4-44dc8171185c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439914} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0e61ef4-813a-54e7-a078-9f5f2ea40a21" :answer-field/key "$(-1,1,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440314} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cfb6205-7f60-523e-9e19-0a6c99cb1be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439915} {:answer/id #uuid "1132f5d8-3334-5ba6-860f-3020fef135aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439916}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439915} :answer-field/id #uuid "47cda995-fd9f-589a-85d9-cfbd97013861" :answer-field/key "$(-1,1,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440315} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "405c5964-ed58-56db-a7d0-221666926760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439917} {:answer/id #uuid "7990ef8e-46b5-5559-a2c5-aa8d65421d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439917} :answer-field/id #uuid "84892d33-55b9-54ef-a343-3de68b52e47d" :answer-field/key "$(1,-1,-1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440316} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d182b83a-f3d7-59a8-a2df-a282497de34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439919} {:answer/id #uuid "767b3c80-fc75-58d7-a96d-c20b801d7033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439920}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439919} :answer-field/id #uuid "71df9b88-565a-50cb-b76e-a7999233dce6" :answer-field/key "$(1,1,1)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c503ae86-f71f-5a21-9c90-e19ce4d410a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71474" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z,\n$$ find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{1}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.\n$$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b898a6-e9f5-51f8-8993-b6f0322af5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(3,2)$ and $(-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403573} {:answer/id #uuid "3acac861-9675-5531-8e0f-29301bd84beb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403574} {:answer/id #uuid "879099ba-26c3-5eca-b156-1546984f09e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403575} {:answer/id #uuid "56e7a278-62d5-5448-adb4-7672d93764ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403576} {:answer/id #uuid "c66b876c-2af7-5756-8122-0570c4918dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403575} :answer-field/id #uuid "22c59322-d13f-5287-a0fa-c2f86091a95e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403572}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "49dc4b38-854b-53b5-ba44-1bf47c27ef1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-72412" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}-4xy=-5{x}^{2}+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy & =-5{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2})j \\\\  & =2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ (2(y-2x)\\,i-(10x-4y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2(y-2x) & =0 \\\\ y & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4xy+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x(2x)+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+5{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d88f68b3-79d9-5a48-a7c2-f15131e65b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403983} {:answer/id #uuid "72609a03-e838-5ef4-baec-8bf52ebe1a8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403984} {:answer/id #uuid "db076067-80ec-504a-b6e7-bc424311aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403985} {:answer/id #uuid "1666ec87-7cf6-516a-a70f-0da860b50921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403986} {:answer/id #uuid "8fb36cf6-f9c5-5daa-a5a9-b549f1488f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403986} :answer-field/id #uuid "72801a88-c8c5-56f3-a725-309aa394dd69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9798ed5-9467-5b84-a966-140686dec581" :question/math-academy-id "q-79281" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac2bba143473bc68e3a578c008a5de3cc36727d984bfba5d5e0da95462c08f77.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c74864b7-21e6-51d9-b31e-e93ec6f64438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403990} {:answer/id #uuid "dfcb7b6f-03d1-5a9f-aaf6-8d93d0e2ba27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403991} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff0c1a4-82c6-5bf6-b268-db87fa75d327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403992} {:answer/id #uuid "f296137c-cb8b-5b96-adf7-adc48bd4980e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403993} {:answer/id #uuid "9d4e132a-2d8b-58bf-90f8-5ab2d0617d3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403990} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffc31ac3-a14f-5473-aab8-9cd20c05206e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03c80413-078e-59d2-b783-1afba7566fa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-79285" :question/problem "![](images/5f/5fb18775e625816f119cf95c09051eba41fced187f4e4d1e7964921f4b877288.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,2),$ $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}]}
